  data:  # be1b
    kind: work_item  # be1b
    title: "javascript: a route marker's dispatches_to resolves a dotted handler reference (user.list) by bare name across files, so `GET /users` in one example dispatches to another example's `list(ids)`"  # be1b
    status: todo_hard  # be1b
    priority: 3  # be1b
    parent:  # be1b
    tags:  # be1b
    - javascript  # be1b
    - analysis_quality  # be1b
    - framework_dispatch_linkers  # be1b
    isbefore: []  # be1b
    duplicate_of: []  # be1b
    not_duplicate_of: []  # be1b
    pr_ref:  # be1b
    description: "PROBLEM (found by WI-sosov's express-repo A/B, 2026-11-04, dev ceca2f496c + the WI-sosov branch; lab ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/sosov_11032026/RESULT.md \"Commit 2 A/B\"). In ~/curriculum_repos/express, `examples/route-separation/index.js:40` registers `app.get('/users', user.list)` (and `:49` `app.get('/posts', post.list)`). The map's route markers `javascript:examples/route-separation/index.js:40-40:GET /users:function` and `...:49-49:GET /posts:function` both carry a `dispatches_to` edge to `javascript:examples/online/index.js:40-44:list:function` -- the `list(ids)` function of a DIFFERENT example -- instead of `user.list` / `post.list` in `examples/route-separation/{user,post}.js`. The handler reference is dotted (`user.list`), and whatever resolved it kept only the bare member name and picked a same-named function elsewhere (the discovery-order / bare-name family, LIVE §5). Measured consequence under WI-sosov's parameter seed: before the same-file guard, the wrong target's first parameter `ids` was seeded as the request and the marker was represented, i.e. taken OUT of the unseeded-ingress disclosure while its real handler was never followed (the false-all-clear direction). WI-sosov now follows a dispatch only into the marker's own file (the Go HandleFunc shape), so cross-file dispatch targets mint no seed at all: a recall gap for Express apps that keep handlers in separate modules (the common `routes/` layout), which this item's fix would close.\n\nALSO SEEN in the same map: `javascript:lib/application.js:1-1:file:file` is a `request_ingress` entry of kind `http_route` (a FILE symbol as a request handler) -- a separate entrypoint-detection oddity, noted here, not sized.\n\nFIX CANDIDATE: resolve a dotted handler reference through the importing file's bindings (`const user = require('./user')` -> the `list` export of `examples/route-separation/user.js`), and refuse a bare-name fallback that lands outside the registering file's import graph; emit no `dispatches_to` rather than a wrong one. Then WI-sosov's same-file guard can widen to \"a target the marker's file imports\". Pre-register with the express repo: the two markers dispatch to `user.js`/`post.js` functions, no marker dispatches outside its file's imports, and the seeds then mint for `user.list(req, res)` et al. (each read against source).\n\nRELATED: WI-sosov (the consumer; same-file guard shipped there), WI-ritog tier 3 note (\"that Express's _cb_* / _GET_*_handler minted symbols carry an inbound edge in every registration shape\" is NOT asserted), the route_handler linker (linkers/route_handler.py, meta[\"handler_ref\"])."  # be1b
    fields: {}  # be1b
